1 | devops | Add a scoped dependabot ignore so TypeScript is held on 6.x, and record why. THE SITUATION, already diagnosed so do not re-derive it: dependabot PR 2786 proposes typescript 6.0.3 to 7.0.2. TypeScript 7 is the rewritten compiler and its JavaScript API surface changed. Exactly one file in the repo imports that API, apps/web/lib/flight-place-contract.test.ts, and it fails to compile under 7 with errors like Property createSourceFile does not exist and Namespace typescript slash lib slash version has no exported member Node. THAT TEST MUST NOT BE TOUCHED, WEAKENED, SKIPPED OR DELETED. It is the guard from issue 2401, where fifteen fixture files carried an inverted airport field and every test stayed green because the fixtures all agreed with each other. It derives the correct direction mechanically from the maxLength in api.yaml rather than from any fixture, which is why it can catch a uniformly wrong fixture layer that tsc cannot see. Your task is CONFIG ONLY. WHAT TO DO: in .github/dependabot.yml, under the existing npm ecosystem entry at directory slash, add an ignore rule for the typescript dependency scoped to MAJOR version updates ONLY. This is the load bearing detail: we must keep receiving typescript 6.x patch and minor bumps, so the rule must use the update-types form that blocks only version-update colon semver-major and must not be a blanket ignore of the package. Verify the exact key names and allowed values against the current dependabot configuration reference rather than writing them from memory, and say in the PR body where you checked, because a malformed ignore block is accepted silently by dependabot and simply does nothing. Match the file existing comment style, which explains WHY a setting exists rather than restating what it does, and keep the comment short. The comment must say: TypeScript 7 changed the compiler JavaScript API, one file uses it, holding on 6 until that is reworked, and name PR 2786 and the flight-place-contract test so the next person can find both. DO NOT add ignore rules for any other package. DO NOT change the schedule, labels or open-pull-requests-limit. DO NOT modify package.json or package-lock.json, and do not upgrade or downgrade anything. VERIFY: confirm the YAML parses, for example with python3 -c import yaml then yaml.safe_load of the file, and paste the real exit code captured directly rather than read after a pipe. Confirm git diff shows exactly one file changed. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash character anywhere in any line you add, including the commit message and PR body. Open a PR. In the PR body state plainly that this is a deliberate hold rather than a fix, that the alternative considered was reworking the test off the compiler API and was rejected as work with no payoff today, and that the hold should be revisited when the ecosystem settles. Omit any Co-Authored-By trailer. Do not merge. Do not close PR 2786: that is a separate step handled outside this task. | ci/hold-typescript-6

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch ci/hold-typescript-6. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: does this ignore rule actually do what it claims, and does it block MORE than it claims. A dependabot ignore block with a wrong key or a wrong value is accepted silently and does nothing at all, so a rule that looks right and is inert would leave us believing the noise is handled while PR 2786 gets reopened next week. FIRST, verify the schema: check every key and value used against the current dependabot configuration reference, and state which keys are valid and where you confirmed them. Do not accept the producer word for it. SECOND, and this is the part that matters most, verify the SCOPE. The rule must block ONLY major version updates for typescript. Confirm that a 6.x patch or minor bump would still be proposed, and say how you determined that. A blanket ignore of the typescript package would silently stop security and bugfix updates to the compiler we actually use, which is a worse outcome than the noise it fixes. THIRD, confirm no other package gained an ignore rule and that schedule, labels and open-pull-requests-limit are unchanged for both ecosystems. FOURTH, confirm apps/web/lib/flight-place-contract.test.ts is UNTOUCHED, and that package.json and package-lock.json are unchanged. If the diff weakens, skips or deletes that test in any way, that is a BLOCK-ESCALATE rather than a BLOCK-FIX: it is the fixture inversion guard from issue 2401 and the whole point of this change is to preserve it. FIFTH, confirm the YAML parses and the diff is exactly one file. SIXTH, confirm no long dash character in any added line, including the PR body and commit message. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC HOLD TYPESCRIPT 6, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | ci/hold-typescript-6
